Evaluation

The Engineer class architecture provides a solid, object-oriented foundation for a node-based sci-fi game, effectively separating positional tracking from player mechanics. By inheriting basic spatial features from the Substance parent class—such as name and current_node—the design ensures clean code reuse while extending functionality with player-specific attributes like plasma_charges and the is_functional lifecycle flag. Furthermore, handling movement through the traverse() method allows player positional updates to be conditionally gated, successfully blocking movement when the character is incapacitated.

However, from a game design perspective, the engine currently has a few key limitations that need addressing in future iterations. The primary issue is that traverse() accepts any MatrixNode without validating whether it is adjacent to the player's current location, allowing illegal "teleportation" across the ship map. Additionally, while resource properties like plasma_charges are defined upon initialisation, active mechanics for spending or managing these charges during gameplay are still missing. Adding graph adjacency checks and dedicated action methods will transform this structural prototype into a fully responsive game loop.
